            "description": "Watch Boca Juniors vs U. Catolica highlights and goals from CONMEBOL Libertadores on ReFooty: Football Highlights Hub. Match played on May 28, 2026. The stage was set for an epic encounter between two giants of South American football in the prestigious CONMEBOL Libertadores . The stands pulsed with the energy of the fans as the players took to the pitch for a duel that promised intensity from the first to the last minute. Montes Strikes in the First Half On May 28, 2026 , the pitch became the setting for a tactical battle where error was not an option. After a balanced start to the match, the scales tipped in favor of the visitors in the 34th minute. In a play of great vision, Eugenio Mena provided the assist for Clemente Montes , who clinicaly found the back of the net to open the scoring for U. Catolica . The frustration of the home side began to show shortly after, with Cristian Cuevas receiving a yellow card just a minute after the goal, reflecting the increasing physical tension of the match. Tactical Adjustments and On-Field Intensity At the restart, Boca Juniors sought to change the course of events, bringing on Alan Velasco for Ander Herrera . However, the Chilean side's defensive organization remained solid, forcing the Argentines into a more aggressive style of play. In the 54th minute, Fernando Zampedri was cautioned with a yellow, followed quickly by Milton Delgado for Boca , during a phase where the referee had to intervene frequently to maintain control. Substitutions followed, with the introduction of names like Angel Romero and Miguel Merentiel in an attempt to break through the defensive blockade. Final Assault and Chilean Resilience In the final moments, Boca Juniors ' pressure intensified, but U. Catolica knew how to endure and manage the clock. Fernando Zampedri and Clemente Montes made way for fresh legs, while the home team tried everything for an equalizer. Deep into stoppage time, in the 95th minute, Lautaro Di Lollo received a yellow card, underlining the desperation of the Argentine side. Despite the final effort and nine minutes of added time, the final whistle confirmed a strategic and historic triumph for the visitors in Buenos Aires."
